<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.6.4: Finding the Missing Numbers in Pairs of Equivalent Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02173</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.6.4: Finding the Missing Numbers in Pairs of Equivalent Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>370353ab-1012-4f85-b2fb-f841d1d1c467</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>The problems below consist of pairs of equivalent expressions: one in standard form and the other in factored form. Note that the leading coefficients on the quadratic terms are all 1.</para>
<para>Find the missing numbers.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2\; +\) __\(x\; +\) __ and \((x + 1)(x + 17)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 18 and 17, respectively. The completed expression is \(x^2+18x +17\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2+9x +14\) and \((x + 2)(x\; +\) __\()\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 7. The completed expression is \((x + 2)(x + 7)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2−7x +10\) and \((x − 2)(x\; −\) __\()\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 5. The completed expression is \((x − 2)(x − 5)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(x^2−9x +20\) and \((x\; −\) __\()(x\; −\) __\()\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 4 and 5. The completed expression is \((x − 4)(x − 5)\).</para>
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>If students make computational errors, address those in the next lesson and emphasize the use of diagrams.</para></section>
</content>
